module GenericConstructionArgumentMonomorphisation

// Pinned limitation, not desired behaviour.
//
// A construction in argument position infers inside a generic function, but the type it
// infers holds the enclosing definition's freshened type variables rather than its generic
// parameters. Monomorphisation cannot always resolve those: the call over the array below
// leaves the element type of `Growable::Append` unresolved.
//
// Binding the construction to an annotated local first compiles and runs - that lands on
// the generic parameters instead. So this is a monomorphisation gap rather than an
// inference one. See test/generics/success/generic_construction_in_call_argument.mmt for
// the shapes that do work end to end.
//
// `Growable` is not the point - it exists only as a vehicle, the way `Appendable` used to
// be before it was deleted for mutating in place. It lives with its one instance and its
// forwarder `AppendTo` in ../generic_construction_argument_monomorphisation_lib.mmt (one
// directory up, not alongside this file - a .mmt directly under a `failure/` directory is
// itself run as its own expected-to-fail test, and the lib module compiles and runs fine
// standalone) so that: (1) `AppendTo` carries no `where` clause, which is what makes
// monomorphisation rather than the type checker responsible for resolving
// `Growable::Append`'s concrete instance, and (2) the forwarder is reached across a module
// boundary, the way the old ArrayUtil forwarder for Append used to be.
//
// The old Appendable-based version of this file additionally pinned a line-misattribution
// bug: the reported line number belonged to the real call site inside ArrayUtil.mmt, but
// the file name and quoted source text were taken from whichever file was compiling, so
// the caret landed on an unrelated line of the test file. That still reproduces here: the
// line number in the .expected snapshot below is the true line of the `Growable::Append`
// call inside the companion lib module, but the quoted source text is this file's own line
// at that same number - unrelated to the call, whatever it happens to be after an edit to
// this comment. Do not "fix" the reported line by rearranging this file; that is the bug.

import
{
	"../generic_construction_argument_monomorphisation_lib.mmt",
}

type Slot<K, V> = Empty | Occupied(K, V);

def CountSlots = fn<K, V>(slots : Array<Slot<K, V>>) -> Int => #slots;

def FillGeneric = fn<K, V>(slots : Array<Slot<K, V>>) -> Int =>
{
	def appended = GenericConstructionArgumentMonomorphisationLib::AppendTo(slots, Slot::Empty());
	CountSlots(appended)
};

def texts : Array<Slot<Text, Int>> = [];

def count = FillGeneric(texts);
